diagnosis_text:
A thorough four-phase diagnostic sweep of the Kath4 lab found no fault consistent with the helpdesk report. In Phase 1, all 15 workstations (dc_pc5, pc1–pc14) responded to cross-VLAN pings with 0% loss, confirming that each host can reach its default gateway and that inter-VLAN routing is intact. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) confirmed that all 15 hosts have valid IPv4 addresses and default routes, no ACL drop rules are present, no static ARP poison entries exist, and no duplicate MAC addresses appear anywhere in the lab. The DOWN interfaces flagged by l2_snapshot are exclusively on collision domains labelled `unused_*` in the topology — baseline-normal for unpopulated ports. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) found all nine FRR devices (router1, router7, router8, router10, router11, router16, router17, mswitch0, sshswitch) operating with Full OSPF adjacencies, 17 routes each, and zero traffic-shaping anomalies. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no CPU, socket, or stress-tool pressure on any device. The sshswitch JSON-collection errors seen in l2_snapshot and tc_snapshot are a known harness length-limit artefact; its FRR/OSPF data was collected cleanly and showed normal operation as the gateway for VLANs 5 and 6. Because a host that cannot reach its gateway is physically incapable of generating a successful cross-VLAN ping, and all 15 hosts did exactly that, the claimed symptom is mechanistically ruled out by live evidence. The fault described in ticket #8129 is unconfirmed — either it was intermittent and self-resolved, or the user's workstation is functioning correctly and the perception of failure has another explanation (e.g. application-layer issue unrelated to IP networking).
=== END ===